      "lineNbr": 60,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958203
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-31T21:31:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The PDL UUIDs are to be used only right at the point of parsing and serializing. You can see that there are Into\u003c\u003e implementations to the regular Uuid type. So the idea is that the Uuid type will be used everywhere, the PDL UUID types should never be used explicitly, and when reading UUIDs out of packets the .into() method will automatically convert into these UUID types. PDL UUIDs are pretty useless (e.g. they don\u0027t support equality/hashing), and converting them into regular Uuids is fallible, so I don\u0027t think they will be inadvertently used when a regular Uuid is needed.\n\nThe idea is that PDL UUIDs represent a serialized UUID within a packet so e.g. a 16-bit and 128-bit UUID will be different, PDL UUIDs just hold raw bytes. Whereas Uuids internally are a \"logical\" UUID (like the UUID type in C++) that doesn\u0027t care about the serialized implementation.",
      "parentUuid": "f57083e0_dc1f1622",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 60,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 60,
        "endChar": 11
      },
      "revId": "1688f96d9127e471ae171c031c8a58677567a6ac",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
